The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2314 (2016), and 2319 (2016),

Condemning in the strongest terms any use of any toxic chemical as a weapon in the Syrian Arab Republic,

Reaffirming that the use of chemical weapons anywhere constitutes a grave violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security,

Reiterating that those individuals, entities, groups, or governments responsible for any use of chemical weapons must be held accountable, and expressing its determination to ensure that perpetrators are brought to justice,

Expressing its continued appreciation for the professional, independent, and impartial work carried out by the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) Fact-Finding Mission in Syria and the OPCW-United Nations Joint Investigative Mechanism,

Taking note of the reports of the Joint Investigative Mechanism, in particular its seventh report dated 26 October 2017 (S/2017/904), presented pursuant to resolution 2319 (2016),

Emphasizing the vital importance of maintaining the independent, impartial, and highly technical character of the Joint Investigative Mechanism in fulfilling its mandate to identify perpetrators of chemical weapons attacks in the Syrian Arab Republic,

1. Decides to renew the mandate of the OPCW-United Nations Joint Investigative Mechanism, as established in resolution 2235 (2015) and extended in resolution 2319 (2016), for a further period of one year from the date of adoption of this resolution;

2. Reaffirms its full support for the Joint Investigative Mechanism, underlines the importance of its independence, credibility, and professional competence, and calls upon all members of the Security Council and the wider international community to respect its impartiality;

3. Calls upon all parties in the Syrian Arab Republic to cooperate fully with the Joint Investigative Mechanism and the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission, in particular by granting full, immediate, and unhindered access to all sites, individuals, documentation, and material deemed relevant by the Mechanism to the conduct of its investigations;

4. Requests the Secretary-General, in coordination with the Director-General of the OPCW, to ensure that the Joint Investigative Mechanism continues to be provided with the necessary financial and material resources, personnel, and technical expertise required to carry out its mandate effectively and safely;

5. Requests the Joint Investigative Mechanism to report to the Security Council and to the OPCW Executive Council every sixty days on the progress of its work, including any challenges encountered in the implementation of its mandate;

6. Reaffirms that the Syrian Arab Republic shall comply fully with all obligations under resolution 2118 (2013) and the Chemical Weapons Convention, and reiterates its determination to impose measures under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations in the event of non-compliance;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

